---
import EnergyReserveForms from "../../../components/studies/EnergyReserveForms.astro";
import VoidCapacitorMounts from "../../../components/studies/VoidCapacitorMounts.astro";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((s) => s.slug === "energy-support-2026-11-02");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<p>
		<strong>Closed as failed by the owner, 2 November 2026.</strong>
		This study stopped during exploration; no visual design was accepted for
		implementation. The subsequent prompt revision clarified the brief for a
		separate cycle. The owner later reported that the other cycle succeeded.
		This page preserves the work of this cycle.
	</p>
	<section>
		<h2>01 / Extra energy needs a character</h2>
		<p>
			A Fighter carries one extra energy pack; a Corvette carries two. The
			equipment increases the ship’s energy storage capacity. The current
			twin-tank drawing supplies a visible fitting, but its vessel-like
			construction is a placeholder for this inquiry. Neither tanks, a battery
			casing nor a final name determines what the object should become.
		</p>
		<p>
			The support family promises “I create opportunities.” Its established
			yellow-green accent (#CEDB17) belongs on connectors, struts and
			attachments; members need not share a silhouette. For extra energy, a
			useful starting hypothesis is
			<strong>held power, ready to become action</strong>. The tension is giving
			that quiet potential a convincing physical presence beside expressive
			engines, without relying on movement, sound or effects to explain it.
		</p>
		<p>
			This study will establish the device’s nature and defining
			characteristics, then derive its name and shape from them. It is a static
			object mounted above or below a ship, built from readable solid parts and
			flat planes. Its construction must remain convincing on Wasp, Scout,
			Fighter and Corvette, enlarged and at flight scale. Charging rules remain
			outside this inquiry. Success means an identifiable energy device that
			belongs to the support family while leaving each ship’s character legible.
		</p>
		<p>
			A ship has 100 intrinsic energy. The normal device adds 85 (185 total);
			the large adds 140 (240 total) and weighs twice as much. These are design
			givens. The two devices must look almost identical, while their shapes
			communicate the larger reserve and heavier construction. Absolute mass
			remains unspecified; the comparison uses m and 2m.
		</p>
		<p>
			<a href="/lab/studies/utility-machinery-2026-11-02#reference"
				>All four ships and four engine references</a
			>
			·
			<a href="/lab/utility-artwork"
				>Current shared utility artwork and mounted storage</a
			>
			·
			<a href="/lab/studies/utility-machinery-2026-11-02#family-colour"
				>Settled family colour reference</a
			>
		</p>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2>02 / Understanding extra energy</h2>
		<details>
			<summary>02.1 / One device, two densities</summary>
			<p>
				The large device holds about 65% more energy for 100% more mass. Simply
				doubling the normal device would suggest the wrong relationship. These
				two construction hypotheses keep the connection and overall identity,
				adding substance to the large version. Names below describe construction
				ideas, not proposed product names.
			</p>
			<EnergyReserveForms />
			<p>
				These are static procedural sketches at common scales, with the
				established support accent on retaining structures. A tests layered,
				tightly packed material; B tests a single contained body. Neither relies
				on glowing energy, a nozzle, animation or sound. Shape can communicate
				relative heft and capacity; exact numbers still need labels.
			</p>
			<p>
				The small views test whether the distinction survives reduction.
				Mounting size and fit are provisional: these isolated objects do not yet
				establish readability above or below a hull. Neither construction is
				selected.
			</p>
			<p>
				<strong>What 02.1 revealed:</strong>
				the comparison was useful because it exposed a missing foundation.
				Capacity and mass relationships alone do not establish what the device
				should communicate or how it belongs in the game’s language. Without a
				shared function, name and character, these illustrations offer mostly
				visual noise rather than a meaningful choice. The sketches remain as
				evidence of that discovery; neither is a direction to refine yet. Step
				02.2 develops that foundation before further visual exploration.
			</p>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section id="meaning">
		<details>
			<summary>02.2 / What stores this much energy?</summary>
			<p>
				The device stores and releases immense amounts of energy. Its
				established function is additional capacity: 85 or 140 beyond the ship’s
				intrinsic 100. Its name and physical nature remain open. Storage
				capacity does not establish charging speed, discharge rate or engine
				output.
			</p>
			<p>
				The following are fictional technology proposals, not scientific
				explanations. Familiar technical words provide associations; the storage
				principles deliberately extend into science fiction. They describe what
				the object could be, before deciding its shape.
			</p>
			<div class="theory-table">
				<table>
					<thead>
						<tr>
							<th>Name</th>
							<th>Fictional storage principle</th>
							<th>Release mechanism</th>
							<th>Character</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						<tr>
							<th>Accumulator</th>
							<td>
								A dense medium absorbs incoming energy into progressively
								excited internal states.
							</td>
							<td>A regulator lets the medium relax in controlled stages.</td>
							<td>
								Industrial, durable, substantial. Names the storage function
								while leaving the medium open.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th>Capacitor</th>
							<td>
								Extreme opposing charges are held apart by an exotic insulating
								material.
							</td>
							<td>
								A controlled connection releases the stored electrical
								potential.
							</td>
							<td>
								Tension held in check; precise and powerful. Suggests rapid
								discharge, which would remain fiction unless separately adopted
								as a mechanic.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th>Energy cell</th>
							<td>
								A sealed active material switches between charged and depleted
								states.
							</td>
							<td>
								An interface converts the material’s state change into usable
								power.
							</td>
							<td>
								Self-contained, standardized, replaceable equipment. Clear name,
								comparatively little implied technology.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th>Energy lattice</th>
							<td>
								An engineered crystal traps energy throughout a network of
								stable microscopic sites.
							</td>
							<td>A tuned signal unlocks sites in sequence.</td>
							<td>
								Ordered, dense, advanced material. The storage substance itself
								has identity.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th>Flux reservoir</th>
							<td>
								A closed field contains circulating energy without allowing it
								to escape.
							</td>
							<td>
								A coupling draws energy out while maintaining confinement.
							</td>
							<td>
								Immense contained activity inside a quiet object. Needs no
								visible circulation or animation.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th>Induction store</th>
							<td>
								A persistent current circulates through an exotic conductor,
								sustaining an exceptionally dense field.
							</td>
							<td>
								Controlled coupling transfers energy from that field into the
								ship.
							</td>
							<td>
								Heavy electrical machinery; continuous containment rather than
								consumable fuel.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th>Phase bank</th>
							<td>
								A material is forced into a metastable phase that retains
								enormous energy.
							</td>
							<td>
								Small regions return to their resting phase under regulation.
							</td>
							<td>
								Dense and inert-looking, with a profound internal change.
								Suggests material engineering rather than a pressure vessel.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th>Potential well</th>
							<td>
								An artificial local field holds an energetic system away from
								its natural equilibrium.
							</td>
							<td>The confinement is eased by measured amounts.</td>
							<td>
								Restrained force; unusual, sophisticated technology. May overlap
								the gravity-device vocabulary.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th>Photon vault</th>
							<td>
								Radiation is trapped within a near-lossless optical confinement
								medium.
							</td>
							<td>
								A controlled aperture converts a fraction of the trapped
								radiation into power.
							</td>
							<td>
								Sealed brilliance, precise containment. Does not require a
								transparent body or visible glow.
							</td>
						</tr>
						<tr>
							<th>Vacuum bank</th>
							<td>
								Supplied energy establishes an artificial excited vacuum state
								inside a bounded chamber.
							</td>
							<td>Controlled collapse returns the stored input energy.</td>
							<td>
								Exotic and formidable. Must remain a finite store, not imply an
								unlimited energy source.
							</td>
						</tr>
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p>
				These names operate at different levels: accumulator describes a
				function, cell a unit, lattice a medium, and capacitor a principle. They
				can combine naturally—“lattice cell,” for example—but a combination
				should make the technology clearer rather than merely sound technical.
				No naming lead is selected.
			</p>
			<h4>Shared characteristics and open choices</h4>
			<p>
				Both sizes use the same technology. The normal device adds 85; the large
				adds 140 at twice the mass. A possible fictional explanation is that
				higher capacity needs disproportionately more containment or stabilizing
				material. That explanation is a hypothesis, not an additional agreed
				property. Absolute mass, storage medium and the nature of containment
				remain open.
			</p>
			<p>
				The object remains static, with no animation or SFX layers. Its
				support-family accent belongs on connectors, struts and attachments.
				Whether the technology feels rugged, refined, volatile or stable should
				be established before its construction is drawn. These fiction proposals
				add no leakage, explosions, charging rules or discharge bonuses to the
				game.
			</p>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section id="void-and-well">
		<h3>02.3 / Void capacitor — lattices of potential</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Void capacitor is the selected name.</strong>
			Its emerging nature combines multiple lattices of potential with void
			wells separating the layers. The precise fictional mechanism remains
			provisional; the exterior is still open.
		</p>
		<blockquote>
			Stores immense potential in interleaved lattices separated by captive void
			wells. The wells hold each layer apart; controlled coupling releases the
			stored energy into the ship.
		</blockquote>
		<small>176 characters · proposed equipment description</small>
		<p>
			Each lattice holds a share of the stored potential. Between them, captive
			void wells maintain separation, allowing immense differences in potential
			across a compact assembly. Charging raises those differences; controlled
			coupling draws them down and feeds energy into the ship. “Void wells”
			describes the separating regions inside the capacitor, rather than another
			equipment type.
		</p>
		<p>
			The defining character is repeated, ordered layers holding extraordinary
			tension in a quiet object. More capacity could require more lattice area
			and stronger confinement, giving a possible reason for the large version’s
			disproportionate mass. Layer count, material and containment construction
			remain hypotheses. Internal layering need not be exposed or dictate a
			stack-shaped exterior.
		</p>
		<h4>The comparison that established the concept</h4>
		<p>
			The two proposals made a useful distinction: the capacitor held electrical
			tension, while the potential well held energy through spatial confinement.
			The resulting direction keeps <strong>Void capacitor</strong> as the
			device name and combines multiple lattices of potential with separating
			void wells. The comparison helped define the device’s nature; its sketches
			did not select an exterior shape.
		</p>
		<div class="concept-pair">
			<article>
				<h4>Void capacitor</h4>
				<blockquote>
					Stores immense electrical potential across a captive void. Its
					containment frame keeps the charged boundaries apart; regulated
					discharge feeds energy back into the ship.
				</blockquote>
				<small>169 characters</small>
				<p>
					The void is an engineered separation between charged boundaries.
					Charging increases their electrical potential; the surrounding frame
					maintains that separation. “Capacitor” makes the object recognizable
					as equipment, while “void” identifies the unusual medium. Its
					character is tension, precision and immense charge held motionless.
				</p>
				<svg
					viewBox="0 0 320 220"
					role="img"
					aria-label="Playful void capacitor sketch: opposing solid banks around a dark gap, joined by yellow-green restraints"
				>
					<path d="M65 54 L125 35 L125 157 L65 176 Z" fill="#819eae" />
					<path d="M65 54 L82 66 L82 187 L65 176 Z" fill="#304759" />
					<path d="M65 54 L125 35 L141 48 L82 66 Z" fill="#d1e5ec" />
					<path d="M183 35 L243 54 L243 176 L183 157 Z" fill="#819eae" />
					<path d="M243 54 L257 67 L257 187 L243 176 Z" fill="#304759" />
					<path d="M183 35 L243 54 L257 67 L198 48 Z" fill="#d1e5ec" />
					<path
						d="M90 70 L215 70 L215 86 L90 86 Z M90 139 L215 139 L215 155 L90 155 Z"
						fill="#cedb17"
					/>
					<path
						d="M90 86 L215 86 L222 93 L97 93 Z M90 155 L215 155 L222 162 L97 162 Z"
						fill="#71780d"
					/>
				</svg>
				<p class="sketch-note">
					Playful sketch: separated masses and physical restraints. No
					construction selected.
				</p>
			</article>
			<article>
				<h4>Potential well</h4>
				<blockquote>
					Stores energy in a pocket of folded space. The confinement core
					deepens the well as it charges, then lets it unwind under load,
					returning the stored energy to the ship.
				</blockquote>
				<small>168 characters</small>
				<p>
					The well is a manufactured pocket of distorted space. Charging forces
					it further from its resting state; controlled relaxation returns that
					input as usable energy. “Well” names the confinement itself. Its
					character is depth, compression and a quiet exterior enclosing
					something disproportionate to its size. The name may need
					differentiation from gravity-charging equipment.
				</p>
				<svg
					viewBox="0 0 320 220"
					role="img"
					aria-label="Playful potential well sketch: nested faceted containment around a dark central recess"
				>
					<path
						d="M160 22 L251 69 L251 158 L160 204 L69 158 L69 69 Z"
						fill="#304759"
					/>
					<path d="M160 22 L251 69 L160 116 L69 69 Z" fill="#d1e5ec" />
					<path d="M69 69 L160 116 L160 204 L69 158 Z" fill="#819eae" />
					<path
						d="M160 46 L224 79 L224 143 L160 176 L96 143 L96 79 Z"
						fill="#cedb17"
					/>
					<path
						d="M160 66 L205 89 L205 132 L160 155 L115 132 L115 89 Z"
						fill="#304759"
					/>
					<path
						d="M160 83 L189 98 L189 124 L160 139 L131 124 L131 98 Z"
						fill="#070710"
					/>
					<path
						d="M150 19 L169 19 L169 52 L150 52 Z M150 173 L169 173 L169 205 L150 205 Z"
						fill="#cedb17"
					/>
				</svg>
				<p class="sketch-note">
					Playful sketch: nested confinement and a dark recess. No literal open
					hole required.
				</p>
			</article>
		</div>
		<p>
			Normal and large remain +85 and +140 capacity, with the large at twice the
			mass. The lattice-and-well fiction may explain this through additional
			containment structure; that explanation is not settled. These sketches
			explore associations only, not mount fit, size variants or final game
			artwork. The device remains static with no animation or SFX layers.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section id="lattice-construction">
		<details>
			<summary>02.4 / Exposed lattices — rejected visual proposal</summary>
			<p>
				<strong>Review result:</strong>
				the drawing did not belong to the support family or express the game’s
				faux-3D low-poly construction at either scale. Its bars and frame are
				not a direction to refine. The name, internal lattice fiction and
				capacity/mass givens remain intact.
			</p>
			<p>
				A first construction hypothesis gives the capacitor three broad lattice
				bodies separated by dark void gaps. Two yellow-green retainers hold the
				assembly, with one common connection. The lattice is an internal
				material, so its drawing uses solid planes rather than a fine mesh that
				would vanish in flight.
			</p>
			<p>
				The normal and large share the same three-part rhythm, connector and
				mounting centre. The large has longer lattice bodies, deeper containment
				and heavier retainers. It is not a uniformly enlarged copy: the common
				interface helps it read as the same equipment. These proportions are a
				hypothesis, not a literal diagram of the 85:140 capacity ratio or
				doubled mass.
			</p>
			<VoidCapacitorMounts />
			<p>
				The fleet uses production hulls, engine artwork and equipment-mount
				coordinates. Both sizes occupy the first equipment mount. Current rules
				put Wasp and Scout equipment below the hull, Fighter and Corvette above
				it; the layer control tests alternatives explicitly. Engine choices
				compare all four engine families. These are artwork fixtures, not
				functional new loadout parts.
			</p>
			<p>
				Shared language comes from assembled solid parts, restrained flat
				facets, left/top lighting and real dark separations. The support accent
				marks containment and attachment. The capacitor has no nozzle, exhaust
				or animated core. Its repeated crosswise gaps distinguish stored
				potential from an engine’s directional outlet.
			</p>
			<p>
				The under-hull views test a real constraint: a concealed centre cannot
				carry identity. The exposed ends and support frame must remain readable,
				or placement and footprint need revision. Enlarged fleet views are 2.3×
				and small views 0.65× at full canvas size. Narrow layouts scale the
				canvases down further. These views support discussion of fit and
				silhouette; they do not establish human acceptance or final proportions.
			</p>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section id="containment-pivot">
		<h3>02.5 / Design the containment, keep the lattices inside</h3>
		<p>
			<strong>Proposed pivot:</strong>
			the void capacitor is a sealed containment assembly. Its internal lattices
			explain how it works; they need not become visible bars. The exterior
			should convey a compact, substantial manufactured object held together
			against immense internal potential.
		</p>
		<table>
			<thead>
				<tr>
					<th>Part</th>
					<th>Concrete construction proposal</th>
					<th>Reason</th>
				</tr>
			</thead>
			<tbody>
				<tr>
					<th>Containment body</th>
					<td>
						One low, solid volume with a raised central ridge, broad sloping
						shoulders and cut-back ends.
					</td>
					<td>
						Large planes and a clear height change establish volume before
						detail. The blunt ends avoid an engine’s outlet or a weapon’s
						barrel.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<th>Structural clamps</th>
					<td>
						Two substantial yellow-green clamps wrap across the body near its
						ends. Each has a top face, turning shoulder and dark side face.
					</td>
					<td>
						The family colour belongs to load-bearing parts with thickness,
						following the support-family attachment language.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<th>Connection</th>
					<td>
						A compact recessed connection beneath the body, shared by both
						sizes.
					</td>
					<td>
						The assembly sits on a ship as equipment; the connector does not
						become its defining silhouette.
					</td>
				</tr>
				<tr>
					<th>Normal / large</th>
					<td>
						Keep the length, clamp positions and connection. Give the large body
						broader shoulders and a higher ridge; enlarge the clamp
						cross-section to suit.
					</td>
					<td>
						The same device becomes more substantial without doubling its parts.
						Wider shoulders provide a top-down size cue; height alone would be
						insufficient.
					</td>
				</tr>
			</tbody>
		</table>
		<p>
			This is one construction hypothesis, not a selected design. The key
			decision is whether a sealed, visibly restrained body expresses the
			capacitor better than exposed internal machinery. If it does, the next
			visual should be one enlarged normal/large pair beside an established
			engine and support item, using their actual flat-plane drawing language.
			Ship mounting follows once that object is convincing.
		</p>
		<p>
			The first mounted comparison remains evidence of the unsuccessful
			construction attempt. It does not settle fit. Under-hull mounting may
			conceal the body, so exposed shoulders and clamp ends will need a separate
			check after the exterior direction is established.
		</p>
	</section>
	<section><h2>03 / To be developed after 02</h2></section>
</StudyLayout>

